---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TextPage from '../components/TextPage.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Accessibility — KAVOVO"
  description="How KAVOVO approaches accessibility, what is in place today, and how to report a problem."
>
  <TextPage
    eyebrow="Accessibility"
    title="Accessibility"
    intro="A recipe is no use if you cannot read it. Here is where we stand."
    updated="2026-08-05"
  >
    <h2>What we aim for</h2>
    <p>
      We target WCAG 2.1 level AA. That is a goal we work towards rather than a certified result, and this page is meant
      to be an honest account rather than a badge.
    </p>

    <h2>In place today</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Semantic headings in order, so screen readers and skim-readers get the same structure.</li>
      <li>A visible focus ring on every link, button and form field for keyboard users.</li>
      <li>The mobile menu is a real button with <code>aria-expanded</code>, and closes with Escape.</li>
      <li>Filter results announce their count to assistive technology as they change.</li>
      <li>Text alternatives on photography; decorative images are hidden from screen readers.</li>
      <li>Body text and controls stay readable down to a 320&nbsp;px viewport, with no horizontal scrolling.</li>
      <li>Animation is disabled for visitors who ask for reduced motion.</li>
      <li>Difficulty and category are always words, never colour alone.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Known gaps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Some recipe photography still uses generic alternative text rather than a full description.</li>
      <li>A few placeholder illustrations remain while final photography is produced.</li>
      <li>We have not yet completed a full audit with screen readers on every page.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Tell us what breaks</h2>
    <p>
      If something is hard or impossible to use, email <a href="mailto:hello@kavovo.coffee">hello@kavovo.coffee</a> with
      the page address and what went wrong. Access problems are treated as bugs, not feature requests, and we will tell
      you what we intend to do about it.
    </p>
  </TextPage>
</Layout>
